          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I can't breathe properly and my inhaler isn't working. My chest feels really tight.",
          "answer": "**Call emergency services now (999 in the UK, 911 in the US, 112 in the EU).** Difficulty breathing that is not improving with an inhaler is an emergency. Do not wait to see if it gets better. (Source: *Severe difficulty breathing*.)\n\nWhile you wait:\n- Sit upright and try to stay calm and still.\n- If you have an asthma reliever plan from your doctor, follow it as written. Do not take extra doses beyond that plan.\n- Ask someone near you to stay with you.\n\nChest tightness with breathlessness can also be a sign of a heart problem, and that is treated as an emergency too, so there is no need to work out the cause first. (Source: *Chest pain that may indicate a heart attack*.)\n\nTell the responders the following. Being able to describe these clearly helps them:\n- When the symptoms started.\n- Whether they are getting worse.\n- Whether you have lips, face or fingertips that look blue or grey.\n- Whether you can speak in full sentences.\n- Any allergic reaction, such as swelling of the lips, tongue or throat, or hives. Some of these can signal a severe allergic reaction, which is also an emergency. (Source: *Anaphylaxis and severe allergic reaction*.)\n\nGet help immediately if you notice any of these: you are gasping, you can only manage a few words at a time, your lips or face turn blue or grey, you become drowsy or confused, or you feel faint.\n\nI can't assess your condition from here, and this information is not a substitute for emergency care.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
